---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    author={author}
    authorUrl={authorUrl}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    headings={headings}
    category="Tools"
    categorySlug="tools"
>
  <h2 id="introduction">Introduction: Video Meetings Are the New Normal</h2>
  <p>
    Video conferencing has transformed from a nice-to-have to an absolute essential for remote teams. Whether you're
    running daily standups, client presentations, or all-hands meetings, the right video conferencing tool can make or
    break your team's communication.
  </p>
  <p>
    But with dozens of options available - from household names like <strong>Zoom</strong> and <strong>Microsoft
    Teams</strong> to newer players like <strong>Around</strong> and <strong>Whereby</strong> - how do you choose?
  </p>
  <p>
    We spent over 40 hours testing 10 video conferencing platforms across different use cases: small team standups,
    large webinars, podcast recordings, and async video messages. We evaluated video/audio quality, ease of use,
    features, pricing, and reliability.
  </p>
  <p><strong>TL;DR - Our Top Picks:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Zoom</strong> - Best overall for most teams (robust features, reliable performance)</li>
    <li><strong>Google Meet</strong> - Best for Google Workspace users (seamless integration)</li>
    <li><strong>Microsoft Teams</strong> - Best for Microsoft 365 users (generous free tier)</li>
    <li><strong>Whereby</strong> - Best for simplicity (no downloads required)</li>
    <li><strong>Loom</strong> - Best for async video communication</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="quick-picks">Quick Picks: Best Video Conferencing Tools at a Glance</h2>
  <ComparisonTable
      columns={quickPicksColumns}
      rows={quickPicksRows}
      caption="Quick comparison of top video conferencing tools"
  />

  <h2 id="what-to-look-for">What to Look For in Video Conferencing Software</h2>
  <p>Before diving into specific tools, here are the key factors we evaluated:</p>

  <h3>1. Video & Audio Quality</h3>
  <p>
    The most important feature. Look for platforms that support <strong>HD video (1080p)</strong> and have good
    automatic audio leveling. Most modern tools handle this well, but performance varies based on your internet
    connection.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Participant Limits</h3>
  <p>
    How many people can join a call? Free tiers typically cap at 100 participants, while paid plans support 300-10,000+
    for webinars. Consider your largest meetings when choosing.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Time Limits</h3>
  <p>
    Many free plans limit group meetings to 40-60 minutes. This is fine for quick syncs but frustrating for longer
    workshops or client calls.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Recording Capabilities</h3>
  <p>
    Can you record meetings? Where are recordings saved (cloud vs local)? Who can access them? Essential for async
    teams and documentation.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Screen Sharing & Collaboration</h3>
  <p>
    All platforms offer basic screen sharing, but look for advanced features like: multiple screen shares, annotation
    tools, remote control, and whiteboarding.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Integrations</h3>
  <p>
    Does it integrate with your existing tools? Calendar integration (Google Calendar, Outlook) is essential. Slack,
    project management tools, and CRM integrations are bonuses.
  </p>

  <h3>7. Security & Privacy</h3>
  <p>
    Look for: end-to-end encryption, waiting rooms, password protection, and the ability to lock meetings. HIPAA/SOC 2
    compliance matters for regulated industries.
  </p>

  <h3>8. Ease of Use</h3>
  <p>
    Can participants join without creating accounts? Is the interface intuitive? Are mobile apps available? The easier
    it is for guests to join, the better.
  </p>

  <h2 id="detailed-reviews">Detailed Reviews: Top 10 Video Conferencing Tools</h2>

  <h3 id="zoom">1. Zoom - Best Overall Video Conferencing Platform</h3>
  <div class="flex gap-4 mt-4">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/zoom" variant="affiliate">Try Zoom Free</AffiliateButton>
  </div>

  <p><strong>Pricing:</strong> Free (40 min limit) | Pro: $15.99/host/mo | Business: $19.99/host/mo</p>
  <p><strong>Free plan participants:</strong> Up to 100</p>
  <p><strong>Paid plan participants:</strong> Up to 1,000 (Zoom Webinar adds up to 10,000+)</p>

  <h4>What We Like</h4>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Industry-leading reliability</strong> - Consistently stable connections with minimal drops</li>
    <li><strong>Robust feature set</strong> - Breakout rooms, polling, whiteboarding, reactions, and more</li>
    <li><strong>Excellent webinar capabilities</strong> - Registration pages, Q&A, panelist controls</li>
    <li><strong>Virtual backgrounds & filters</strong> - Best-in-class background replacement technology</li>
    <li><strong>Recording options</strong> - Local recording (free) or cloud recording (paid)</li>
    <li><strong>Strong mobile apps</strong> - Full-featured iOS and Android apps</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>What Could Be Better</h4>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>40-minute limit on free plan</strong> - Can be disruptive for longer team meetings</li>
    <li><strong>Breakout rooms require paid plan</strong> - A feature that should be available on free tier</li>
    <li><strong>"Zoom fatigue" stigma</strong> - Some people are burned out on Zoom specifically</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>Best For</h4>
  <p>
    Teams that need reliable video conferencing with advanced features. Perfect for agencies, consultants, educators,
    and anyone running webinars or large meetings.
  </p>

  <h3 id="google-meet">2. Google Meet - Best for Google Workspace Users</h3>
  <div class="flex gap-4 mt-4">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/google-meet" variant="affiliate">Try Google Meet</AffiliateButton>
  </div>

  <p><strong>Pricing:</strong> Free (60 min limit) | Workspace Individual: $7.99/mo | Business: $12/user/mo</p>
  <p><strong>Free plan participants:</strong> Up to 100</p>
  <p><strong>Paid plan participants:</strong> Up to 500 (Enterprise)</p>

  <h4>What We Like</h4>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Seamless Google integration</strong> - Works perfectly with Gmail, Calendar, and Drive</li>
    <li><strong>No app required</strong> - Runs smoothly in Chrome browser</li>
    <li><strong>60-minute free meetings</strong> - More generous than Zoom's 40 minutes</li>
    <li><strong>Simple, clean interface</strong> - Less overwhelming than feature-heavy competitors</li>
    <li><strong>Good mobile experience</strong> - Lightweight apps that don't drain battery</li>
    <li><strong>Live captions</strong> - Built-in speech-to-text (English only)</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>What Could Be Better</h4>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Recording requires paid plan</strong> - No recording on free tier</li>
    <li><strong>Limited advanced features</strong> - No whiteboarding, fewer integrations than Zoom</li>
    <li><strong>Breakout rooms only on paid plans</strong> - And less intuitive than Zoom's implementation</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>Best For</h4>
  <p>
    Remote teams already using Google Workspace. If your calendar, email, and docs are in Google, Meet is the obvious
    choice for video calls.
  </p>

  <h3 id="microsoft-teams">3. Microsoft Teams - Best Free Plan</h3>
  <div class="flex gap-4 mt-4">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/microsoft-teams" variant="affiliate">Try Teams Free</AffiliateButton>
  </div>

  <p><strong>Pricing:</strong> Free (60 min limit) | Essentials: $4/user/mo | Business: $12.50/user/mo</p>
  <p><strong>Free plan participants:</strong> Up to 100</p>
  <p><strong>Paid plan participants:</strong> Up to 10,000</p>

  <h4>What We Like</h4>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Most generous free tier</strong> - Recording, breakout rooms, and 60-minute meetings all free</li>
    <li><strong>All-in-one platform</strong> - Video, chat, file sharing, and project management in one app</li>
    <li><strong>Microsoft 365 integration</strong> - Perfect for Outlook, OneDrive, and Office users</li>
    <li><strong>Large meeting capacity</strong> - Supports up to 10,000 participants on enterprise plans</li>
    <li><strong>Good security features</strong> - Enterprise-grade encryption and compliance</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>What Could Be Better</h4>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Cluttered interface</strong> - Too many features can be overwhelming for simple video calls</li>
    <li><strong>Resource-heavy</strong> - Desktop app uses more CPU/RAM than lightweight competitors</li>
    <li><strong>Better for internal teams</strong> - External guests may find it confusing</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>Best For</h4>
  <p>
    Organizations using Microsoft 365, larger enterprises, and teams that want an all-in-one collaboration platform.
    Also great for budget-conscious teams due to the generous free tier.
  </p>

  <h3 id="webex">4. Webex - Best for Enterprise Security</h3>
  <div class="flex gap-4 mt-4">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/webex" variant="affiliate">Try Webex Free</AffiliateButton>
  </div>

  <p><strong>Pricing:</strong> Free (40 min limit) | Starter: $14.50/host/mo | Business: $29.95/host/mo</p>
  <p><strong>Free plan participants:</strong> Up to 100</p>
  <p><strong>Paid plan participants:</strong> Up to 1,000</p>

  <h4>What We Like</h4>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Enterprise-grade security</strong> - End-to-end encryption, SOC 2, HIPAA compliance</li>
    <li><strong>Excellent for large webinars</strong> - Supports up to 100,000 participants</li>
    <li><strong>Cisco reliability</strong> - Rock-solid infrastructure backed by enterprise networking leader</li>
    <li><strong>Good AI features</strong> - Background noise removal, gesture recognition</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>What Could Be Better</h4>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Outdated interface</strong> - Feels less modern than Zoom or Meet</li>
    <li><strong>Expensive for small teams</strong> - Pricing is geared toward enterprise budgets</li>
    <li><strong>Steep learning curve</strong> - More complex than consumer-friendly alternatives</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>Best For</h4>
  <p>
    Large enterprises with strict security requirements, especially in healthcare, finance, and government sectors.
  </p>

  <h3 id="gotomeeting">5. GoToMeeting - Best for Webinars</h3>
  <div class="flex gap-4 mt-4">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/gotomeeting" variant="affiliate">Try GoToMeeting</AffiliateButton>
  </div>

  <p><strong>Pricing:</strong> Professional: $14/organizer/mo | Business: $19/organizer/mo | No free plan (14-day
    trial)</p>
  <p><strong>Participants:</strong> Up to 250</p>

  <h4>What We Like</h4>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Professional webinar features</strong> - Registration, polls, handouts, analytics</li>
    <li><strong>Drawing tools</strong> - Better annotation and whiteboarding than most competitors</li>
    <li><strong>Personal meeting rooms</strong> - Consistent URLs for recurring meetings</li>
    <li><strong>Good mobile apps</strong> - Includes the "GoTo" app for iOS/Android</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>What Could Be Better</h4>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>No free plan</strong> - Only a 14-day trial</li>
    <li><strong>More expensive than Zoom</strong> - For similar features</li>
    <li><strong>Dated branding</strong> - Feels less modern than newer competitors</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>Best For</h4>
  <p>Consultants, coaches, and businesses that run frequent webinars and need professional hosting features.</p>

  <h3 id="around">6. Around - Best for Async Teams</h3>
  <div class="flex gap-4 mt-4">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/around" variant="affiliate">Try Around</AffiliateButton>
  </div>

  <p><strong>Pricing:</strong> Pro: $10/user/mo | Business: $16/user/mo | No free plan (14-day trial)</p>
  <p><strong>Participants:</strong> Up to 50</p>

  <h4>What We Like</h4>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Floating bubbles</strong> - Video appears as small circles, not full-screen takeover</li>
    <li><strong>Multiple screen shares</strong> - Everyone can share screens simultaneously</li>
    <li><strong>Low resource usage</strong> - Uses significantly less CPU than Zoom</li>
    <li><strong>Beautiful, modern design</strong> - Feels fresh compared to traditional video calls</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>What Could Be Better</h4>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>No free plan</strong> - Only 14-day trial</li>
    <li><strong>Limited to 50 participants</strong> - Not suitable for large meetings</li>
    <li><strong>Fewer features</strong> - No recording, breakout rooms, or webinar capabilities</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>Best For</h4>
  <p>Small, design-forward teams that prefer casual, less invasive video communication.</p>

  <h3 id="whereby">7. Whereby - Best for Simplicity</h3>
  <div class="flex gap-4 mt-4">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/whereby" variant="affiliate">Try Whereby Free</AffiliateButton>
  </div>

  <p><strong>Pricing:</strong> Free (4 participants) | Pro: $9.99/host/mo | Business: $15.99/host/mo</p>
  <p><strong>Free plan participants:</strong> Up to 4</p>
  <p><strong>Paid plan participants:</strong> Up to 200</p>

  <h4>What We Like</h4>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>No downloads required</strong> - Runs entirely in the browser</li>
    <li><strong>Custom room URLs</strong> - Get a permanent link like whereby.com/yourname</li>
    <li><strong>Beautiful interface</strong> - Clean, minimalist design</li>
    <li><strong>Embed in website</strong> - Can add video calls directly to your site</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>What Could Be Better</h4>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Free plan only allows 4 participants</strong> - Very limited</li>
    <li><strong>No recording on lower tiers</strong> - Only available on Business plan</li>
    <li><strong>Fewer features than competitors</strong> - No breakout rooms or advanced tools</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>Best For</h4>
  <p>1:1 calls, small team meetings, therapists, coaches, and anyone who wants the simplest possible video calling
    experience.</p>

  <h3 id="discord">8. Discord - Best for Creative Teams</h3>
  <div class="flex gap-4 mt-4">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/discord" variant="affiliate">Try Discord Free</AffiliateButton>
  </div>

  <p><strong>Pricing:</strong> Free | Nitro: $9.99/mo (optional, for perks)</p>
  <p><strong>Participants:</strong> Up to 50 in video (unlimited voice)</p>

  <h4>What We Like</h4>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Completely free</strong> - No time limits, no participant limits (for voice)</li>
    <li><strong>Persistent channels</strong> - Always-on voice/video rooms for drop-in collaboration</li>
    <li><strong>Great for gaming/creative teams</strong> - Screen sharing with audio is excellent</li>
    <li><strong>Community features</strong> - Forums, roles, and community management tools</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>What Could Be Better</h4>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Not professional-looking</strong> - Gaming-focused branding doesn't suit corporate clients</li>
    <li><strong>No native recording</strong> - Requires bots or third-party tools</li>
    <li><strong>Video limited to 50 people</strong> - Voice is unlimited, but video has caps</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>Best For</h4>
  <p>
    Creative teams, gaming studios, developer communities, and anyone who wants persistent voice/video channels for
    spontaneous collaboration.
  </p>

  <h3 id="loom">9. Loom - Best for Async Video</h3>
  <div class="flex gap-4 mt-4">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/loom" variant="affiliate">Try Loom Free</AffiliateButton>
  </div>

  <p><strong>Pricing:</strong> Free (25 videos, 5 min each) | Business: $12.50/creator/mo</p>
  <p><strong>Use case:</strong> Async video messages (not live calls)</p>

  <h4>What We Like</h4>
  <ul>
