  <h2 id="free-vs-paid">Free vs Paid: Do You Need to Upgrade?</h2>
  <p>Here's when it makes sense to upgrade from a free plan:</p>

  <h3>Stick with Free If:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Your meetings are under 40-60 minutes</li>
    <li>You have fewer than 100 participants</li>
    <li>You don't need cloud recording</li>
    <li>Breakout rooms aren't essential</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Upgrade to Paid If:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>You run long meetings</strong> - Training sessions, workshops, or client calls over an hour</li>
    <li><strong>You need recording</strong> - For compliance, training, or documentation</li>
    <li><strong>You use breakout rooms</strong> - For workshops, education, or collaborative sessions</li>
    <li><strong>You have large meetings</strong> - Over 100 participants regularly</li>
    <li><strong>You run webinars</strong> - Registration, Q&A, and analytics require paid plans</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Best value paid plans:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Zoom Pro ($15.99/host/mo)</strong> - Best overall features for the price</li>
    <li><strong>Microsoft Teams Essentials ($4/user/mo)</strong> - Cheapest option with good features</li>
    <li><strong>Google Workspace Individual ($7.99/mo)</strong> - Great if you need other Google tools</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="integration-capabilities">Integration Capabilities</h2>
  <p>Video conferencing tools don't exist in isolation. Here's how they integrate with popular tools:</p>

  <h3>Calendar Integration</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Google Meet</strong> - Native Google Calendar integration (obviously)</li>
    <li><strong>Microsoft Teams</strong> - Native Outlook integration</li>
    <li><strong>Zoom</strong> - Works with Google Calendar, Outlook, and most calendar apps via plugins</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Slack Integration</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Zoom</strong> - Start/join meetings directly from Slack with /zoom command</li>
    <li><strong>Google Meet</strong> - Start calls with /meet in Slack</li>
    <li><strong>Whereby</strong> - One-click meeting links in Slack</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Project Management Tools</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Zoom</strong> - Integrates with Asana, Monday.com, Trello</li>
    <li><strong>Microsoft Teams</strong> - Deep integration with Microsoft Planner</li>
    <li><strong>Loom</strong> - Embed videos in Notion, ClickUp, Jira</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="security-features">Security and Privacy Features</h2>
  <p>Security is critical, especially if you're discussing sensitive information. Here's what to look for:</p>

  <h3>End-to-End Encryption (E2EE)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Zoom</strong> - Offers E2EE (must be manually enabled)</li>
    <li><strong>Whereby</strong> - E2EE for all calls</li>
    <li><strong>Most others</strong> - Use TLS encryption (secure, but not E2EE)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Meeting Security Controls</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Waiting rooms</strong> - All major platforms support this</li>
    <li><strong>Password protection</strong> - Standard across all tools</li>
    <li><strong>Lock meetings</strong> - Prevent new participants from joining mid-call</li>
    <li><strong>Remove participants</strong> - Host controls to remove disruptive users</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Compliance Certifications</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>HIPAA compliant</strong> - Zoom, Webex, Google Meet (Workspace only), Microsoft Teams</li>
    <li><strong>SOC 2 certified</strong> - All major platforms</li>
    <li><strong>GDPR compliant</strong> - All major platforms</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
  <FAQSection faqs={faqs}/>

  <h2 id="final-recommendations">Final Recommendations: Which Tool Should You Choose?</h2>
  <p>Here's our final advice based on your specific needs:</p>

  <h3>For Most Teams: Zoom</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Zoom</strong> remains the gold standard for a reason. It's reliable, feature-rich, and everyone knows how to
    use it. The 40-minute limit on free plans is annoying, but <strong>Zoom Pro at $15.99/host/month</strong> is worth
    it for most professional teams.
  </p>

  <h3>If You Use Google Workspace: Google Meet</h3>
  <p>
    The integration with Gmail and Google Calendar makes <strong>Google Meet</strong> a no-brainer for teams already in
    the Google ecosystem. It's simpler than Zoom and has a generous 60-minute free tier.
  </p>

  <h3>If You Use Microsoft 365: Microsoft Teams</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Microsoft Teams</strong> offers incredible value, especially on the free plan. If you're already paying for
    Microsoft 365, Teams is included and beats most competitors' paid plans.
  </p>

  <h3>If You Want Simplicity: Whereby</h3>
  <p>
    For small teams, coaches, or anyone who wants zero-friction video calls, <strong>Whereby</strong> can't be beat. No
    downloads, beautiful interface, custom URLs. Perfect for 1:1s and small groups.
  </p>

  <h3>If You Run Webinars: Zoom or GoToMeeting</h3>
  <p>
    Both <strong>Zoom</strong> and <strong>GoToMeeting</strong> excel at webinars with registration, Q&A, polls, and
    analytics. Zoom is more popular, GoToMeeting has better drawing tools.
  </p>

  <h3>If You Want to Go Async: Loom</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Loom</strong> isn't a Zoom replacement - it's a meeting reducer. Use it to record updates, demos, and
    feedback instead of scheduling yet another call. Your team will thank you.
  </p>

  <h3>If You're Budget-Conscious: Discord or Microsoft Teams Free</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Discord</strong> is 100% free with unlimited calling (50 video participants). <strong>Microsoft Teams
    Free</strong> offers 60-minute meetings with recording and breakout rooms. Both are incredible value.
  </p>

  <p class="mt-8"><strong>Bottom line:</strong> You can't go wrong with <strong>Zoom</strong>, <strong>Google
    Meet</strong>, or <strong>Microsoft Teams</strong>. Choose based on which ecosystem you're already using. For most
    independent teams, <strong>Zoom Pro</strong> is the best investment.</p>

